//kage:unit pixels

package main

// The rounded picture shader of gift: a texture seen through the distance
// field of shape.kage.
//
// A picture with square corners does not come here. It is a textured quad
// drawn by Ebitengine's own linear filter, which is the cheapest thing a GPU
// can do with a texture. This shader exists for the one thing that path cannot
// do, which is to give the picture the antialiased rounded corners of an
// OpFillRoundRect of the same bounds and radius — so that a gallery tile keeps
// its shape when its placeholder turns into its photograph, and so that the
// selection border drawn on top of it covers its edge instead of framing a
// square that pokes out of the border's corners.
//
// # Vertex layout
//
//	src0Pos  texel position, plus the texture's atlas origin, which
//	         Ebitengine adds on the way in
//	color    premultiplied tint
//	custom.x q.x of the distance field, device pixels; see below
//	custom.y q.y
//	custom.z corner radius, device pixels, already clamped by the CPU side
//	custom.w unused, zero
//
// No uniforms, for the reason shape.kage states: Ebitengine's Uniforms is a
// map[string]any and writing it allocates once per draw.
//
// # Why the distance field arrives half evaluated
//
// The rounded box of shape.kage needs the position inside the box, its two
// half extents and the radius — five numbers — and a textured vertex has only
// the four custom slots left once the texture coordinate and the tint are in
// place. Glass solved the same shortage by quantising three scalars into one
// slot; a half extent cannot be quantised like that, because the edge of the
// picture would move by the rounding.
//
// So the CPU side does not send the position at all. It sends
//
//	q = |p| - halfExtent + radius
//
// which is the first line of sdRoundBox, and it can, because it emits the
// picture as four quads, one per quadrant of the box. Inside one quadrant the
// sign of p is fixed, so |p| is p or -p and q is an *affine* function of the
// position — exactly what a vertex attribute interpolates without error. What
// is left for the fragment is the second line of sdRoundBox, which needs
// nothing but q and the radius. The cost is twelve more vertices per picture,
// which is nothing next to the fragments; the quad split is on
// Renderer.appendRoundImage.
//
// # Filtering is done here, because Ebitengine will not do it for a shader
//
// Ebitengine samples a Kage source nearest, always; kawase_down.kage explains
// where in the pinned driver that is decided. A thumbnail is scaled to
// whatever its tile happens to be, which is why the square image path uses a
// linear filter, and a rounded picture drawn nearest would visibly differ from
// its square neighbour. So bilinear below is the four fetch blend of
// Ebitengine's own linear filter (internal/builtinshader of the pinned
// module): the same taps and the same weights. Unlike that filter it clamps
// every tap to the texture's own region, because the antialiasing pad of a
// rounded picture reaches a pixel beyond its bounds, and whatever lies there
// on a shared atlas page must not bleed into the edge.
//
// # No screen space derivatives
//
// Same as shape.kage: every geometric value arrives in device pixels, so the
// antialiasing band is the constant one pixel and this shader needs no fwidth.

// bilinear is the linear filter of the square image path, written out.
func bilinear(pos vec2) vec4 {
	origin := imageSrc0Origin()
	lo := origin + vec2(0.5)
	hi := origin + imageSrc0Size() - vec2(0.5)
	p := pos - vec2(0.5)
	f := fract(p)
	p0 := floor(p) + vec2(0.5)
	// Clamped to the texel centres of the texture's own region, so every
	// fetch below is inside it and the unchecked fetch is safe.
	c00 := imageSrc0UnsafeAt(clamp(p0, lo, hi))
	c10 := imageSrc0UnsafeAt(clamp(p0+vec2(1, 0), lo, hi))
	c01 := imageSrc0UnsafeAt(clamp(p0+vec2(0, 1), lo, hi))
	c11 := imageSrc0UnsafeAt(clamp(p0+vec2(1, 1), lo, hi))
	return mix(mix(c00, c10, f.x), mix(c01, c11, f.x), f.y)
}

func Fragment(dstPos vec4, src0Pos vec2, color vec4, custom vec4) vec4 {
	q := custom.xy
	radius := custom.z

	// The second line of sdRoundBox in shape.kage, whose first line the CPU
	// side already evaluated per vertex; see the header.
	d := length(max(q, vec2(0))) + min(max(q.x, q.y), 0.0) - radius

	// The coverage ramp of shape.kage, one device pixel wide and centred on
	// the boundary, so a rounded picture and a rounded fill of the same
	// bounds cover the same pixels to the same degree.
	cov := clamp(0.5-d, 0.0, 1.0)
	if cov <= 0.0 {
		// The antialiasing pad and the outside of the corners: no fetch.
		return vec4(0)
	}
	// Texel, tint and coverage are all premultiplied, so a plain product
	// keeps the result premultiplied.
	return bilinear(src0Pos) * color * cov
}
